  "answer": "No. The Australian dollar is strong against the yen right now, even though it has fallen a bit over the past six weeks.\n\n**Latest rate:** A$1 bought **¥109.67** on 16 October 2026, the most recent RBA reading (4pm AEST).\n\n**Compared with recent history:**\n- **One year ago:** ¥100.84 at the end of October 2025, so the dollar is up about **9%**.\n- **Recent low:** ¥91.43 at the end of April 2025, so it's up about **20%** since then.\n- **Lowest month-end since 2023:** ¥89.10 in March 2023.\n- **Recent high:** ¥114.63 on 1 September 2026, the strongest daily rate in this data. Month-ends were around ¥114 in April, May and August 2026.\n\n**The recent dip:** Since 1 September the dollar has lost about **4.3%** against the yen. It fell to about ¥110 by mid-September and has stayed between ¥109.4 and ¥110.7 through October. So it's a bit weaker than its peak, but only slightly.\n\n**Longer view:** The RBA data I can access only goes back to 2023. From memory, before that the dollar rarely bought more than about ¥105–107, around 2007 and 2013. If that's right, today's level is high by historical standards, not low.\n\nThis means yen-priced costs, like a trip to Japan or Japanese imports, are still cheap for Australians compared with most of the past few years. They're just a little dearer than they were in late August.",
